1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
CareOneX has two halves.

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:04,096
One half talks to a family on the phone.

3
00:00:04,416 --> 00:00:06,888
The other half runs long before any call:

4
00:00:06,888 --> 00:00:09,963
it builds the library
that every answer comes from.

5
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:14,654
This episode is about the library:
five containers

6
00:00:14,654 --> 00:00:19,319
that turn 20 public documents
into 232 searchable passages.

7
00:00:19,567 --> 00:00:21,274
Why build a library at all?

8
00:00:21,594 --> 00:00:24,261
When a caller asks about an income limit,

9
00:00:24,261 --> 00:00:29,466
the agent, the AI assistant on the phone,
must answer from an official document.

10
00:00:29,786 --> 00:00:34,265
It is not allowed to answer from memory,
because these numbers change every year.

11
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,273
So every answer starts
as one of these passages.

12
00:00:37,593 --> 00:00:41,583
Let's follow a document
through the five containers, one at a time.

13
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:46,301
Everything starts with one spreadsheet,
the source list.

14
00:00:46,621 --> 00:00:49,928
It names 20 public documents
that the team approved.

15
00:00:50,167 --> 00:00:51,231
More than half come

16
00:00:51,231 --> 00:00:53,751
from New Jersey's Division
of Aging Services.

17
00:00:54,071 --> 00:00:59,084
The rest come from Medicaid, Medicare,
Veterans Affairs and Disability Services.

18
00:00:59,404 --> 00:01:01,885
11 are web pages, 8 are PDFs,

19
00:01:01,885 --> 00:01:07,532
and 1 is a summary the team wrote
from the state's 2026 documents.

20
00:01:07,767 --> 00:01:10,358
Each row carries what an answer needs later:

21
00:01:10,358 --> 00:01:13,949
the program, the web
address, the date the rules took effect,

22
00:01:13,949 --> 00:01:16,599
and a fingerprint of the file,
called a hash.

23
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:19,478
The source list
is the pipeline's only input.

24
00:01:19,798 --> 00:01:22,678
To add a document,
you add a row and rebuild,

25
00:01:22,678 --> 00:01:26,391
because the list is built
into the data container's image.

26
00:01:27,433 --> 00:01:29,652
S3 is Amazon's file storage.

27
00:01:29,972 --> 00:01:35,241
A bucket is one top-level folder in it,
and each file inside is called an object.

28
00:01:35,561 --> 00:01:39,038
The first container,
data, makes sure the team's bucket exists.

29
00:01:39,358 --> 00:01:41,732
It keeps old versions of every file,

30
00:01:41,732 --> 00:01:45,161
it is encrypted,
and it holds public documents only.

31
00:01:45,481 --> 00:01:48,041
No caller information ever goes into it.

32
00:01:48,283 --> 00:01:51,184
Its name starts with ac215 on purpose.

33
00:01:51,504 --> 00:01:56,466
The team's S3 permissions only reach buckets
whose names start with ac215,

34
00:01:56,466 --> 00:02:01,092
so nothing in this course can touch
the company's production buckets,

35
00:02:01,092 --> 00:02:02,768
which are named careonex.

36
00:02:03,016 --> 00:02:04,331
The second container,

37
00:02:04,331 --> 00:02:07,773
ingest, downloads every document
and computes its hash.

38
00:02:08,093 --> 00:02:11,464
If the bucket already holds a file
with that hash, it skips it.

39
00:02:11,784 --> 00:02:13,960
Otherwise it uploads the new version.

40
00:02:14,583 --> 00:02:18,231
Next to every file it writes
a small sidecar of metadata:

41
00:02:18,231 --> 00:02:21,623
the program,
the year, the state, the effective date.

42
00:02:21,943 --> 00:02:25,868
Later, search can filter on these fields,
for example only JACC.

43
00:02:26,116 --> 00:02:30,852
Every run also writes a manifest:
exactly which file versions it saw.

44
00:02:31,172 --> 00:02:34,202
That manifest is the version
of the whole dataset.

45
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:38,109
Here is something we tested
while making this video.

46
00:02:38,429 --> 00:02:40,733
We downloaded every document twice,

47
00:02:40,733 --> 00:02:43,762
a few seconds apart,
the same way ingest does.

48
00:02:44,082 --> 00:02:46,008
All 8 PDFs came back identical,

49
00:02:46,008 --> 00:02:48,989
and still matched their hash
in the source list.

50
00:02:49,309 --> 00:02:54,066
But 5 of the 11 web pages came back
with different bytes the second time.

51
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:56,689
The text on those pages had not changed.

52
00:02:57,009 --> 00:03:00,833
On nj.gov,
a bot-protection script with a random number

53
00:03:00,833 --> 00:03:02,919
is sometimes added at the end.

54
00:03:03,239 --> 00:03:05,633
On va.gov, a fresh random code,

55
00:03:05,633 --> 00:03:09,959
called a nonce,
is stamped on dozens of tags every time.

56
00:03:10,279 --> 00:03:14,439
So the hash of a downloaded file
cannot tell you whether the rules changed.

57
00:03:14,683 --> 00:03:16,219
That is extract's job.

58
00:03:16,539 --> 00:03:20,253
It turns every file into clean text,
written as Markdown:

59
00:03:20,253 --> 00:03:24,880
plain text with a few symbols for structure,
like a # before a heading.

60
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:29,574
The hash of that text is the real signal
that a document's content moved.

61
00:03:29,817 --> 00:03:32,590
Getting web pages right took three versions.

62
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:36,568
Version 1 used a library
that guesses the main content,

63
00:03:36,568 --> 00:03:39,694
and it silently dropped eligibility
paragraphs.

64
00:03:40,014 --> 00:03:42,722
Version 2 deleted navigation first,

65
00:03:42,722 --> 00:03:47,265
but nj.gov wraps the whole page
inside its navigation bar,

66
00:03:47,265 --> 00:03:49,145
so pages came out empty.

67
00:03:49,465 --> 00:03:53,518
Version 3 finds the main region first,
and only then strips.

68
00:03:53,767 --> 00:03:58,098
PDFs go through a library
called pymupdf4llm.

69
00:03:58,418 --> 00:04:02,078
It keeps headings,
and it turns tables into Markdown tables,

70
00:04:02,078 --> 00:04:04,945
so a number stays
in the same row as its label.

71
00:04:05,983 --> 00:04:09,076
Search should not hand
the agent a 22-page guide.

72
00:04:09,396 --> 00:04:12,414
It should return a passage
short enough to read at once,

73
00:04:12,414 --> 00:04:14,730
and specific enough to answer one question.

74
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:19,018
So the chunk container cuts every document
into pieces called chunks.

75
00:04:19,266 --> 00:04:21,585
Rule one: a heading starts a new chunk,

76
00:04:21,585 --> 00:04:24,557
and every chunk carries
its full path of headings.

77
00:04:24,877 --> 00:04:31,170
So the line "income limit 4,855 dollars"
never travels without the word JACC.

78
00:04:31,416 --> 00:04:33,229
A table stays in one chunk.

79
00:04:33,549 --> 00:04:37,013
Only a table bigger than the cap is split,
between rows,

80
00:04:37,013 --> 00:04:39,672
with its first row repeated on every piece.

81
00:04:39,992 --> 00:04:44,313
Long text is split
at paragraph or sentence breaks,

82
00:04:44,313 --> 00:04:48,973
aiming for about 1,600 characters,
with a cap of 2,800.

83
00:04:49,293 --> 00:04:52,878
Scraps under 200 characters
are merged into a neighbour.

84
00:04:53,116 --> 00:04:56,807
On all 20 documents this gives 232 chunks.

85
00:04:57,127 --> 00:05:01,243
The two longest PDFs,
the program guide and the Medicare booklet,

86
00:05:01,243 --> 00:05:03,015
make up almost half of them.

87
00:05:03,335 --> 00:05:05,852
Most chunks are far below the target:

88
00:05:05,852 --> 00:05:10,204
the median is about 850 characters,
because headings come often.

89
00:05:10,450 --> 00:05:13,885
Each chunk is stored as its own file,
with its own sidecar.

90
00:05:14,205 --> 00:05:18,854
That sidecar must stay under 1 kilobyte:
Bedrock silently skips any chunk

91
00:05:18,854 --> 00:05:22,930
whose metadata is bigger,
and still reports the job as complete.

92
00:05:23,967 --> 00:05:27,978
The last container,
kb-sync, turns every chunk into a vector.

93
00:05:28,298 --> 00:05:32,569
An embedding model,
Amazon Titan Text Embeddings v2,

94
00:05:32,569 --> 00:05:36,020
reads the chunk and returns 1,024 numbers.

95
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:37,843
Titan runs on Bedrock,

96
00:05:37,843 --> 00:05:42,420
Amazon's service for using AI models
without running them yourself.

97
00:05:42,667 --> 00:05:47,638
Think of those numbers as a direction
in a space with 1,024 axes.

98
00:05:47,958 --> 00:05:51,413
Chunks with similar meaning
point in similar directions,

99
00:05:51,413 --> 00:05:53,511
and the angle between two of them,

100
00:05:53,511 --> 00:05:57,152
measured by cosine similarity,
is how search compares them.

101
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:00,103
The vectors live in an S3 Vectors index,

102
00:06:00,103 --> 00:06:03,821
a store built for searching vectors,
called program-kb.

103
00:06:04,141 --> 00:06:08,771
On top sits a Bedrock Knowledge Base
whose data source is the chunks folder.

104
00:06:09,091 --> 00:06:10,928
Its own chunking is switched off,

105
00:06:10,928 --> 00:06:13,934
because our chunk container has already done
that job.

106
00:06:14,184 --> 00:06:17,960
kb-sync starts an ingestion job
and waits for it to finish.

107
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:21,506
Then it writes the knowledge
base's ID into the bucket,

108
00:06:21,506 --> 00:06:24,146
which is how the retrieve
container finds it.

109
00:06:24,384 --> 00:06:25,856
Why S3 Vectors?

110
00:06:26,176 --> 00:06:27,924
It costs cents a month,

111
00:06:27,924 --> 00:06:32,789
while the alternatives start
at about 45 or 175 dollars a month.

112
00:06:33,109 --> 00:06:34,260
The price is speed:

113
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:37,168
about a tenth
to a third of a second per search,

114
00:06:37,168 --> 00:06:40,213
and on a phone call
every tenth of a second counts.

115
00:06:41,250 --> 00:06:43,469
Two habits run through the whole pipeline.

116
00:06:43,789 --> 00:06:48,077
Each container owns one folder of the bucket
and leaves the others alone.

117
00:06:48,397 --> 00:06:50,981
And each one skips work
that is already done,

118
00:06:50,981 --> 00:06:53,623
so running everything again
is cheap and safe.

119
00:06:53,867 --> 00:06:55,211
Now, what is open.

120
00:06:55,531 --> 00:06:58,091
Three things turned up
while we made this video.

121
00:06:58,411 --> 00:07:02,827
First, one page, Veterans Affairs'
Home and Community Based Services,

122
00:07:02,827 --> 00:07:06,347
comes out of extract
as little more than the site menu.

123
00:07:06,667 --> 00:07:10,204
Its list of services sits
in an element named flex-menu,

124
00:07:10,204 --> 00:07:13,109
and the rule
that strips menus throws it away.

125
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:15,377
Second, two sources disagree.

126
00:07:15,697 --> 00:07:20,838
The JACC web page caps services
at 1,156 dollars a month.

127
00:07:21,158 --> 00:07:24,111
The state's 2026 side-by-side table,

128
00:07:24,111 --> 00:07:28,049
and the team's summary built from it,
say 1,090.

129
00:07:28,369 --> 00:07:33,041
Both are 2026, so "prefer
the newest year" cannot settle it.

130
00:07:33,283 --> 00:07:36,142
Third, that same table hides
its program names.

131
00:07:36,462 --> 00:07:39,470
They survive extraction,
in the table's second row.

132
00:07:39,790 --> 00:07:43,864
But when the chunker splits a big table,
it repeats only the first row.

133
00:07:44,184 --> 00:07:48,217
So the chunk
that holds 1,090 dollars never says JACC.

134
00:07:48,467 --> 00:07:53,139
An update from the same evening:
Marco's branch works on open item 3.

135
00:07:53,459 --> 00:07:57,010
Its new chunker repeats the row
with the program names,

136
00:07:57,010 --> 00:08:00,691
and we checked
that the 1,090 dollar chunk now says JACC.

137
00:08:01,011 --> 00:08:04,111
But his new table reader
fails on the real PDF,

138
00:08:04,111 --> 00:08:07,606
and retrieve now drops
the table's unverified chunks,

139
00:08:07,606 --> 00:08:11,102
so for now the table
does not reach the agent at all.

140
00:08:11,422 --> 00:08:14,024
Open items 1 and 2 are unchanged there.

141
00:08:14,344 --> 00:08:16,221
Episode 6 has the details.

142
00:08:16,467 --> 00:08:20,405
Also open: nothing re-runs
this pipeline on a schedule yet,

143
00:08:20,405 --> 00:08:23,208
and the team's summary is updated by hand.

144
00:08:23,450 --> 00:08:25,306
Three questions to test yourself.

145
00:08:25,626 --> 00:08:29,231
Why does extract hash the text
and not the downloaded file?

146
00:08:29,551 --> 00:08:32,133
Why is the heading path
stamped on every chunk?

147
00:08:32,453 --> 00:08:35,100
And when one new PDF is added to the list,

148
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:37,999
which containers do real work
on the next run?

149
00:08:38,233 --> 00:08:42,046
Next episode:
what happens when a question arrives,

150
00:08:42,046 --> 00:08:46,532
and how retrieve finds the right 5 passages
among these 232.

